    q: "When should you use an online feature store vs offline feature store? Trade-offs for a fraud detection system.",
    a: "Approach: Choose based on latency requirement, feature freshness, and operational complexity. For fraud detection: two components: (1) pre-training pipeline (offline), (2) real-time scoring (online). Offline feature store: stores pre-computed features (user 30-day transaction count, average amount, geographic spread) in data warehouse. Accessed during model training. Latency: OK if <6 hours (batch, asynchronous). Training jobs can query 30-day feature matrix (100GB) offline. Cost: cheap (batch Spark jobs). Online feature store: caches hot features in Redis for real-time serving. During fraud check, need to know: \"Is this user's spending pattern normal?\" (computed in <100ms). Example feature: user_30day_spend (computed nightly, cached in Redis, refreshed every 5 minutes via Kafka). Latency requirement: <100ms. If offline only, would need to query warehouse (5-10 second latency) = too slow for payment. Trade-offs: Online stores (Redis) add complexity (keeping cache warm, handling misses), cost (Redis cluster $5K/month). Offline stores simpler but can't serve real-time. Hybrid approach: pre-compute 100 hot features in online store (20GB Redis), rely on offline for training. For fraud specifically: (1) feature importance check: which features matter most for model? (2) identify hot vs cold features. High-cardinality features (user_id-specific spending) = online. Low-cardinality (merchant_category frequency) = batch. Success: online latency <100ms, refresh lag <5min, false positive rate <2%."
