    q: "Choosing between TensorFlow, Triton, and custom APIs for serving a complex ML model. Decision framework.",
    a: "Approach: Evaluate based on model complexity, team expertise, latency SLA, and operational burden. TensorFlow Serving: native TensorFlow model serving (SavedModel format). Pros: simple (no custom code), model versioning built-in, auto-batching. Cons: TensorFlow-only, less control over optimization, slower than Triton (10-20% overhead). Triton (NVIDIA): model-agnostic inference server (supports TensorFlow, PyTorch, ONNX, custom). Pros: 30% faster inference than TensorFlow, multi-model support (serve 10 models on 1 GPU), ensemble support (chain models). Cons: steeper learning curve, more config. Custom API (FastAPI + Python): write your own inference server. Pros: maximum control, model-agnostic, can optimize for your specific model. Cons: you maintain it (bug fixes, security patches), slower unless carefully optimized. Decision framework: (1) Model complexity: single model → TensorFlow OK. Multiple models or ensembles → Triton. (2) Latency SLA: <50ms → Triton. <200ms → TensorFlow. (3) Team expertise: Python-heavy team → FastAPI. ML Ops team → Triton. (4) Operational support: no ML Ops team → TensorFlow (less config). Have ML Ops → Triton (worth complexity). Example: fraud detection model (single DNN, <50ms latency, 10K RPS) → Triton (optimized inference speed + batching). NLP ensemble (3 models, must be chained) → Triton (native ensemble). Custom scientific model → custom FastAPI. Success metric: latency <SLA, operational overhead <10% engineer time."
