    a: "Approach: Kubernetes + resource quotas with priority-based scheduling and preemption. Step 1: Cluster setup: 30 NVIDIA A100 GPUs in Kubernetes (6 nodes, 5 GPUs each). Define resource requests: per job, specify GPU count (1-4), memory (32-64GB), CPU (4-8). If job requests 4 GPUs, Kubernetes schedules on nodes with 4 free GPUs. Step 2: Namespaces + quotas: segment by team (team_data: max 10 GPUs/day, team_recs: max 12 GPUs/day, team_fraud: max 8 GPUs/day). Hard limits prevent one team monopolizing. Step 3: Priority queues: production retraining (priority 1000, immediate) > research experiments (priority 100, queued). If production job arrives and all GPUs busy, preempt lowest-priority job (checkpoint saved, can resume later). Step 4: Autoscaling: if queue backlog >5 hours, emit alert (consider renting cloud GPUs: AWS EC2 p3 instances). Budget: own 30 GPUs (capital cost), rent additional on-demand (variable cost) when queue grows. Step 5: Allocation fairness: track usage per team, enforce fairness across jobs. If team_data uses 10 GPUs and team_recs waiting, preempt team_data's lowest-priority job. Fair-share scheduler (dominant resource fairness). Metrics: average queue wait <1 hour, GPU utilization 75%+, preemption-caused retraining <10% of jobs. Trade-offs: preemption adds complexity (checkpoint/resume logic) but increases utilization 20%; simpler queue (no preemption) less efficient. Success: 80% GPU utilization, <1 hour queue wait, 90% jobs complete without preemption."
