    q: "Design a canary deployment strategy for ML models where you gradually shift traffic, detect issues, and auto-rollback.",
    a: "Approach: Traffic-based canary with continuous monitoring and automated rollback thresholds. Step 1: Canary phases: Day 1: 1% traffic (100 users) to new model v2.0, 99% to current v1.8. Day 2: 5%. Day 3: 10%. Day 7: 100%. Ramp slower (1% per day) for high-risk models, faster (10% per day) for low-risk. Step 2: Monitoring: during each phase, measure: latency (p50, p99), error rate, accuracy (if labels available), user satisfaction (session duration, bounce rate). Alert thresholds: if p99 latency increases >15%, auto-rollback. If error rate >1%, auto-rollback. If accuracy drops >0.5%, auto-rollback. Step 3: Statistical power: with 1% traffic, need 3-7 days to reach statistical significance for small lift (1-2%). Explicitly require 7-day canary for 2% lift claims (reduces false positives). Step 4: Rollback trigger: if any metric crosses threshold, immediately switch 100% traffic back to v1.8. Alert incident (PagerDuty). Step 5: Automation: no manual gate needed between phases (auto-ramp 1% per day). Human can manually halt at any phase (SLA miss, concerning trend). Post-canary: run post-mortem if any issue found, retrain if needed. Use feature flags to control traffic splitting (fast toggle without re-deploying). Metrics: canary duration 7 days, rollback latency <5 min, false positive rate <5% (metrics cross threshold without real issue). Trade-offs: slow canary (7 days) delayed delivery but safer. Fast canary (1 day) riskier. Success: 100% safe deployments, <5 min rollback, zero customer impact from bad models."
