    q: "Design a shadow mode testing strategy for ML models to validate accuracy before production deployment.",
    a: "Approach: Parallel execution where new model processes requests alongside production model, results compared but not used. Step 1: Shadow infrastructure: modify inference pipeline to call both models (v1.8 current, v2.0 candidate). v1.8 result returned to user (no impact). v2.0 result logged asynchronously (no latency overhead). Step 2: Comparison: daily, compare v1.8 and v2.0 outputs on logged 1-day sample (10K predictions). Compute: agreement rate (% predictions agree), confidence distribution (do models equally confident?), drift (does v2.0 output distribution differ?). Step 3: Metrics: if agreement <90% on held-out test, investigate. If agreement >98%, likely very similar models. Ideal: 95-97% agreement (meaningful improvement, not totally different). Step 4: Edge case discovery: shadow mode catches failure modes: what if user sends corrupt data? Both models should handle gracefully. If v2.0 crashes and v1.8 handles, flag before prod. Step 5: Duration: shadow 1 week (covers weekday + weekend pattern variance). If metrics look good, manual review by ML engineer (spot-check 100 mismatches). Then canary. Shadow cost: 2x inference (both models run), adds latency overhead if not async (mitigate with async logging). Metrics: agreement rate, inference latency overhead (<1%), coverage (% requests shadowed). Trade-offs: shadow adds cost and complexity but critical for high-impact models (fraud, recommendations). Low-impact (internal analytics model) can skip shadow. Success: catch 90% of issues pre-canary, zero customer-impacting model bugs."
